# CLAUDE.md — database (migrations / factories / seeders)

Domain invariants for the tables live in `app/CLAUDE.md` and `app/Models/CLAUDE.md`.

## Standard table structure

```php
$table->prefixedId();              // string(20): {ID_PREFIX}_{16 base-62}, e.g. bd_7Kq2mZp9Xt4LwRc1
$table->timestamps();
```

- Foreign keys are `foreignPrefixedId('x_id')->constrained()` with an explicit `cascadeOnDelete()` /
  `restrictOnDelete()`, never the default left implicit.
- Both macros live in `AppServiceProvider::boot()`. `prefixedId()` adds the primary key as a command (not a fluent
  modifier), so a self-referencing foreign key in the same `create` works.
- `users` is the skeleton's bigint (`foreignId('user_id')`). Vendor-owned tables keep the vendor's schema and bigint
  ids: `passkeys`, `personal_access_tokens`, `jobs`, `job_batches`, `failed_jobs`, `cache`, `cache_locks`, `sessions`,
  `password_reset_tokens`.
<!-- if:tenancy -->
- A tenant-owned table adds `$table->tenantId()`, and a key between two tenant-owned tables is composite (Tenancy,
  below). The one vendor-table change is `personal_access_tokens.tenant_id`.
<!-- endif -->
- **bigint exception:** a hot table (tens of millions of rows, never exposed) may use `$table->id()`, with a comment
  on that line giving the reason.
- **Ids are random, never an order.** A table whose rows need write order (feeds, keyset cursors, "latest wins") uses
  `$table->timestampsTz(6)`, and its model sets `protected $dateFormat = 'Y-m-d H:i:s.uO'` so Eloquent keeps the
  microseconds. Order and keyset by `(created_at, id)`, the id only breaking ties.
- Every column a query filters or sorts by gets an index in the same migration.
- A table recording an observation of external state adds
  `$table->timestampTz('observed_at')->index();`: when the state was OBSERVED, not inserted.
- **Append-only tables** (`app/Models/CLAUDE.md`): no in-place edits, no soft or hard deletes. A correction
  is a new row with a `supersedes_id` back-link.
- **High-volume event tables** are short-lived or live outside Postgres; keep only aggregates here.
- Runtime flags (`visibility`, feature toggles) are **columns with a default**.

**Social sign-in (when the project builds it)**, root `CLAUDE.md`, Auth:
- a change migration makes `users.password` `->nullable()->change()`;
- `social_accounts`: `prefixedId()`, `foreignId('user_id')->constrained()->cascadeOnDelete()`, `provider`
  `string(32)`, `provider_user_id` string, `email` nullable (as reported at link time, never used for matching),
  timestamps, `unique(['provider', 'provider_user_id'])`, `unique(['user_id', 'provider'])`;
- provider tokens only when the project calls the provider's API: `text` columns with the `encrypted` cast.

## Migrations

- One concern per migration, named for what it does. Never edit a migration that has run anywhere but your machine;
  add a new one.
- Every migration comes from `php artisan make:migration`; never type or edit its timestamp. When main gains a newer
  migration before yours merges, regenerate yours and move the body.
- Secrets at rest: a `text` column with the `encrypted` cast on the model.
- Postgres everywhere, tests included: a migration never branches on `DB::getDriverName()`. Partitions,
  `pg_trgm`/GiST indexes, `CREATE EXTENSION` and materialised views are written plainly ("today" in a view is
  `CURRENT_DATE`, never a binding).
- Partitioned tables: `bigserial`, not `IDENTITY` (PG 16 partitions don't inherit identity); the primary key includes
  the partition key; never `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY` inside a migration.
- Modifying a column restates ALL its attributes, or they are dropped.
- State-machine columns (`status`, `visibility`, …) are **not mass-assignable**: writes go through the model's
  transition method.
- Migrations run at container start, before the app serves (Hosting): one that locks a big table for minutes is an
  outage, so backfill in a job. A data-fix migration never runs `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` inline (it blocks reads);
  it dispatches the refresh job.
- Indexes are proven: an EXPLAIN justifies a new one, and a zero-scan index in `pg_stat_user_indexes` gets a drop
  migration. A composite `(a, b)` does not serve `WHERE b = ?` on PG 16.

## Factories and seeders

| Seeder | Runs | Content |
|---|---|---|
| `DatabaseSeeder` | `db:seed` | `ReferenceDataSeeder`, then `ProductionSeeder` in production, else `DevSeeder` |
| `ReferenceDataSeeder` | every boot, every seed, the tests (`#[Seeder]`) | `database/data/<table>.json`, or `database/data/<table>/<slug>.json` for tables many branches add to. No faker |
| `DevSeeder` | local and testing only (`LogicException` elsewhere) | `fake()->seed(20260928)`; `admin@{{app}}.test`, `dev@{{app}}.test` / `password`; demo content |
| `ProductionSeeder` | prod boot with `DATABASE_SEED=true` | the operator, every admin (below); no reference data |

- **`ProductionSeeder`** creates the operator from `OPERATOR_EMAIL`/`OPERATOR_PASSWORD` first (the password applies
  only on creation; skipped with a warning if unset), then each missing `ADMIN_EMAILS` account, verified, with a
  random password (set through password reset). It never modifies an existing account. For an unverified one it
  warns: confirm who holds the address, then remove the account and seed again, or verify it by hand.
- **Every seeder is idempotent**: a second `db:seed` that throws is a bug. `DevSeeder` writes users by
  `updateOrCreate` on email, non-fillable columns via `forceFill`.
- **Reference ids are explicit** Stripe-style ids (2–3 lowercase letters + `_` + 16 characters), never changed. The
  seeder rejects a row without one: `upsert` would invent a different id per stack.
- **Loading is `upsert` on `id`, 500 rows per chunk**: no casts, events or `$fillable`; the seeder `json_encode`s
  arrays. A table of ids only loads with `insertOrIgnore`. `database/data/` always exists (`.gitkeep`).
- Tables load in one transaction, **parents first**: the seeder orders them by their foreign keys (a cycle throws), so
  a child file may sort before its parent's. A self-referencing key is not ordered: list rows parent-first.
- **No timestamps on reference tables** (git is their history). A table the product edits at runtime is not reference
  data.
- **No deletion by omission**: removing a reference row is a migration.
- **A change to `database/data/` keeps every existing `id`**: `git diff main -- database/data` shows no removed or
  changed `id`. A removed row, or a slug or unique value moved to another id, comes with its data-fix migration in the
  same commit. An importer that regenerates these files reads the existing ids and keeps them.
- **Admins before roles exist**: `config('auth.admins')` from `ADMIN_EMAILS`, created verified for the `viewHorizon`
  gate (`app/Providers/CLAUDE.md`). When roles land, only the gate body and the seeders change.
- Every model gets a factory with **named states** for the lifecycle (`->pending()`, `->archived()`), so tests never
  hand-set a status. Factories are the sanctioned way to seed non-fillable state.
- **Production seeders never touch factories or `fake()`**: Faker is `--dev`, so the container would crash-loop.
- Never put real credentials, emails or external identifiers in a seeder or factory.
<!-- if:tenancy -->

## Tenancy

**Global tables** (no RLS):
- `tenants`: `prefixedId()`, `name`, timestamps.
- `tenant_user`: `foreignPrefixedId('tenant_id')->constrained()->cascadeOnDelete()`,
  `foreignId('user_id')->constrained()->cascadeOnDelete()`, `role`, timestamps; primary key `(tenant_id, user_id)`;
  index `user_id`.
- `users`.
- `personal_access_tokens`. Its non-null `tenant_id`
  (`foreignPrefixedId('tenant_id')->constrained()->cascadeOnDelete()`) comes in its own migration dated after
  `create_tenants_table`, never in the older `install:api` migration. A table that already holds tokens is emptied
  first.

**Every tenant-owned table** calls `$table->tenantId()` in its create migration. That one command adds:
- a non-null `tenant_id`: `foreignPrefixedId` to `tenants`, `restrictOnDelete()` (an offboarding job removes a
  tenant, never a cascade);
- `unique(['tenant_id', 'id'])`: the leading `tenant_id` index and the composite foreign-key target;
- `ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` and `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`;
- `CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON … FOR ALL USING (tenant_id = nullif(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), ''))`.
  `USING` alone also guards inserts and updates, so a write into another tenant errors. Unset and reset both mean no
  rows.

**Keys between tenant-owned tables are composite**, because foreign-key checks and cascades ignore RLS:
`foreignPrefixedId('project_id')` (no `constrained()`), then
`foreign(['tenant_id', 'project_id'])->references(['tenant_id', 'id'])->on('projects')`. On delete: cascade or
restrict only; `nullOnDelete()` would null `tenant_id`, so use a raw `ON DELETE SET NULL (project_id)`.

**Macros**, in `AppServiceProvider::boot()` next to `prefixedId()`:
- `Blueprint::macro('tenantId', …)` adds the column and the unique, then `$this->addCommand('tenantPolicy')`;
- `PostgresGrammar::macro('compileTenantPolicy', fn (Blueprint $blueprint, Fluent $command) => …)` returns the three
  statements, and `[]` unless `$this instanceof PostgresGrammar` (grammar macros are shared by every grammar).

Other rules:
- A table that becomes tenant-owned later gets the column, backfill, unique and policy in its own migration.
- Every index on a tenant-owned table but the primary key leads with `tenant_id`, and every unique includes it.
- Reference tables are global.
- **Tenant-owned defaults** (rows each tenant starts with) come from one idempotent action that writes a tenant's
  defaults inside `CurrentTenant::run`. Tenant creation calls it. The migration that adds such a table dispatches a job
  that runs it for every existing tenant, never inserts itself: FORCE RLS refuses rows written with no tenant set.

**Views, materialised views and partitions** are granted to `{{app}}_app` like tables; only the queried table's own
policy binds:
- A view over tenant-owned rows is `CREATE VIEW … WITH (security_invoker = true)`.
- A materialised view has no RLS. It carries `tenant_id` and a unique index (for the CONCURRENTLY refresh), and its
  migration runs `REVOKE ALL ON <mv> FROM {{app}}_app`. The app reads it through a `WITH (security_barrier)` view with
  `WHERE tenant_id = nullif(current_setting('app.tenant_id', true), '')`.
- Only the owner may refresh it, so the migration also adds
  `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION refresh_<mv>() RETURNS void LANGUAGE sql SECURITY DEFINER SET search_path = public AS 'REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY <mv>'`,
  revokes EXECUTE from `PUBLIC` and grants it to `{{app}}_app`. The refresh job and tests run
  `select refresh_<mv>()`. `OR REPLACE`, because `migrate:fresh` drops no functions.
- Partitions are created only by migrations, each followed by `REVOKE ALL ON <partition> FROM {{app}}_app`; reads and
  writes go through the parent.
- No other `SECURITY DEFINER` function: it runs as the owner and skips RLS.

**Roles** (Hosting): the tables belong to `{{app}}`; the app connects as `{{app}}_app`. Every `migrate*` command takes
`--database=pgsql_owner`; app code never uses that connection.

**Seeders:**
- Tenant-owned rows are written only inside `CurrentTenant::run($tenant->id, …)`. Seeders work both as the app role
  (`db:seed`) and as the owner (the tests' `migrate:fresh`).
- `DevSeeder` stays idempotent: two tenants by `firstOrCreate(['name' => …])`; `admin@{{app}}.test` in both (to
  exercise the switcher), `dev@{{app}}.test` in one; memberships by `syncWithoutDetaching`; demo rows only when their
  tenant `wasRecentlyCreated`.
- `ProductionSeeder` creates no tenant.
<!-- endif -->
